@startuml error-two-roads
title The two roads - the one thing to remember about package error

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor<<road>> #EEF6FF
  BackgroundColor<<shared>> #FFF3D6
  BackgroundColor<<stop>> #FFE3E3
}
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11

rectangle "REQUEST" as Req

rectangle "Security filter chain\n(SecurityWebFilterChain)" as Sec <<stop>>

rectangle "DispatcherHandler\nrouting, body binding, @Valid,\ncontroller, service, gateway" as Disp

rectangle "ROAD B\nApiAuthenticationEntryPoint  -> 401\nApiAccessDeniedHandler       -> 403" as RoadB <<road>>
rectangle "SecurityErrorWriter\nserialises and writes bytes by hand" as Writer <<road>>

rectangle "ROAD A\nGlobalExceptionHandler\n7 handlers -> respond()" as RoadA <<road>>

rectangle "ErrorResponseFactory\nbuilds the body, adds traceId" as Factory <<shared>>

rectangle "ErrorCode\nstatus + default message" as Code <<shared>>

rectangle "one JSON shape\ntimestamp, path, status,\nerror, message, traceId, details" as Out

Req --> Sec
Sec --> RoadB : rejected\n(only ever 401 or 403)
Sec --> Disp : permitted
Disp --> RoadA : anything thrown\n400 404 405 409 415 500 503
RoadB --> Writer
Writer --> Factory
RoadA --> Factory
Code --> Factory : read
Factory --> Out

note right of Sec
  The watershed. Everything is decided
  by one question: did the filter chain
  let the request through?
end note

note right of Writer
  No message converter runs inside a
  filter, so status, Content-Type,
  serialisation and buffer release are
  all done by hand.
end note

note bottom of Factory
  Two roads, four callers, one builder.
  Nothing else is allowed to assemble
  an ErrorResponse.
end note

@enduml
